WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:04.600
This is a chase scene from 1924.

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:06.550
It's from the movie "Sherlock Jr.,"

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:10.788
and in it, Buster Keaton seems
to almost get hit by a train.

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:15.490
With extremely limited special effects,

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:18.200
the filmmakers had to come
up with an innovative way

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:19.850
to create the illusion.

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:22.280
So they shot the sequence backwards,

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:24.720
then reversed the film.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.518
Since then, Hollywood has developed

00:00:26.518 --> 00:00:28.435
countless ways to make car chases

00:00:28.435 --> 00:00:31.135
feel more real and visceral to audiences,

00:00:31.135 --> 00:00:33.710
from modifying cars to placing cameras

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:35.600
right in the seat of the action.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.460
And without each of these innovations,

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:40.290
we wouldn't have gotten
to the ultimate spectacle

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.440
that is the "Fast &amp; Furious" franchise.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.010
Let's take a look at how
the car chase has evolved

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:47.973
over 100 years in Hollywood.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:51.470
This is the scene that set the standard

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:53.300
for all modern car chases.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:56.530
It's from the 1968 Steve
McQueen movie "Bullitt,"

00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:59.210
and it's iconic partly
because of the characters --

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:01.050
Bullitt: Look, Chalmers,
let's understand each other.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:01.920
I don't like you.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.210
Narrator: But also because of their cars.

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:07.070
This mean-looking Dodge
Charger is a muscle car,

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:09.300
a type of car that exploded in popularity

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.501
during the late '60s in the US.

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:16.420
This new, sleeker generation
was basically built

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:18.260
for informal drag racing,

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:21.490
with big V-8 engines and rear-wheel drive.

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:24.920
The hero car in "Bullitt"
was a Ford Mustang,

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:26.680
the first major pony car --

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:29.700
a more compact, sporty
take on the muscle car.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:30.950
And it's this combination

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:33.700
of streamlined design and V-8 engines

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:37.100
that made these cars powerful
and maneuverable enough

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.100
to pull off daring stunts,

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:41.440
like racing through the
hills of San Francisco

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:43.447
at 110 miles per hour.

00:01:43.447 --> 00:01:45.320
"Bullitt" also had another advantage,

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:47.590
smaller and more rugged cameras,

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:50.400
which allowed shooting to
happen in actual streets.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:51.487
Before this movie,

00:01:51.487 --> 00:01:54.148
most directors shot chase
scenes on studio sets,

00:01:54.148 --> 00:01:57.250
since they were dealing with
cameras that looked like this.

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:58.280
To fill in the backdrop,

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:00.280
filmmakers would use rear projection,

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:01.635
where you project a moving image

00:02:01.635 --> 00:02:03.430
onto a screen behind the actor.

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:04.610
Like in this chase scene

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:06.760
from the first Bond movie, "Dr. No,"

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:08.563
where Sean Connery's steering

00:02:08.563 --> 00:02:11.347
doesn't seem to match the
movement of his convertible.

00:02:11.347 --> 00:02:13.960
"Bullitt" was one of the
first big Hollywood movies

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:17.770
to rely on the lighter
Arriflex 35 II camera,

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.340
so the crew could shoot
entirely on location

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:24.002
and also take audiences inside the cars.

00:02:24.002 --> 00:02:25.701
They mounted cameras on the hood,

00:02:25.701 --> 00:02:27.843
sides, and interiors of each vehicle,

00:02:27.843 --> 00:02:31.001
so viewers could feel
every bump and lurch.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:35.610
This dynamic camera style
was most famously used

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:38.240
in the chase from the movie
"The French Connection."

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:39.820
The filmmakers mounted cameras

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:42.060
on the hood, front bumper, and dashboard

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.060
of Gene Hackman's Pontiac.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:45.270
These multiple angles,

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:46.961
along with handheld-camera work

00:02:46.961 --> 00:02:48.270
from the backseat of the car,

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:51.000
showed all the action from
the driver's perspective.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.504
The result makes that Bond
chase look like a casual spin.

00:02:54.504 --> 00:02:57.490
[Bond theme plays]

00:02:57.490 --> 00:03:01.250
But higher-stakes car cases
can also be more dangerous.

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:03.220
"The French Connection" chase was filmed

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:05.280
on uncleared streets in New York,

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:08.640
without permits, among
other drivers and passersby.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.620
These barely avoided collisions?

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:11.930
They happened for real.

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:14.660
And this crash was entirely unplanned.

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:17.260
At the wheel of the white
Ford was a civilian driver

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:19.290
who was uninvolved in the shoot.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:22.410
All this happened even with
actors sometimes driving slower

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:23.900
than what you see on film,

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:26.250
a standard trick used to reduce risk.

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:28.040
See how some cars' exhaust pipes

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:29.990
are blowing smoke faster than normal?

00:03:29.990 --> 00:03:32.110
That's because filmmakers
on "French Connection"

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:34.660
used a technique known as undercranking,

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:37.510
where they film some of the
scene at a lowered frame rate.

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:40.330
Capturing fewer frames makes
movement look faster --

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:42.380
basically the opposite of slow motion.

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:44.440
You can also see this
in "Vanishing Point,"

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:45.620
released the same year.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:46.810
Barry Newman's character

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:48.710
bets he can drive his Dodge Challenger

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:52.460
from Denver to San Francisco in 15 hours.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:55.690
That'd require a constant
pace of some 80 miles an hour.

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:59.140
But in many sequences, like
this hot race with a Jaguar,

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:01.200
the actors were only driving at 50.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.860
So the filmmakers created
the illusion of speed

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:06.990
by undercranking the camera
to half its normal frame rate,

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:09.119
making the cars look like they're flying.

00:04:09.119 --> 00:04:11.280
[engines roar]

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.380
Undercranking is still used today,

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:17.110
most notably in the 2015
movie "Mad Max: Fury Road."

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:19.770
But heavy undercranking
works best for settings

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:22.287
that don't have a lot
of people moving around.

00:04:22.287 --> 00:04:25.070
"Mad Max" and "Vanishing
Point" were perfect fits,

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:27.490
with scenes that take place in the desert.

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:29.510
In scenes that have lots of pedestrians,

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:32.800
the effect can make human
movement look jerky and fake,

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:35.170
like what you see in old silent comedies.

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:36.470
So city chase scenes

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:38.543
can only use the technique sparingly.

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:42.270
The Paris chase in the
1998 thriller "Ronin,"

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:45.470
where Robert De Niro's
Peugeot pursues a BMW,

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:47.340
didn't use undercranking at all.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:49.670
To actually drive at
these daredevil speeds,

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:52.000
you pretty much always
need a stunt driver.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:53.670
So the filmmakers have to find ways

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:55.070
to make it look like the actor,

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:56.250
not the stunt driver,

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:57.730
is controlling the car.

00:04:57.730 --> 00:05:00.420
In "Ronin," they had right-hand-drive cars

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:03.080
fitted with fake
left-side steering wheels.

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:05.280
The actor, in this case Robert De Niro,

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:07.290
would pretend to drive for the close-ups,

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:08.930
while the stunt driver to their right

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:10.793
did all the actual maneuvering

00:05:10.793 --> 00:05:12.890
on a steering wheel
positioned out of shot.

00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:15.280
But having the stunt
performer in the other seat

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:17.120
limits the angles you can shoot.

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:20.470
And what if the stunt driver
can't be in the vehicle at all?

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:22.070
The solution came from a movie

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:24.945
with exactly zero car chases:

00:05:24.945 --> 00:05:26.428
"Seabiscuit."

00:05:26.428 --> 00:05:28.900
The stunt team on that
movie built a special rig

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:31.030
for filming horse-race scenes.

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:33.400
Eventually, that rig
evolved into a smaller,

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:35.720
more versatile version
designed specifically

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:37.450
for filming car chases.

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:38.840
Known as the Biscuit,

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:40.106
it's basically a vehicle

00:05:40.106 --> 00:05:42.100
that you can put other vehicles on,

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:45.020
one that can be reassembled
into different configurations,

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:46.980
like a giant Lego truck.

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:49.660
The rig makes it look like an
actor is doing the driving,

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:53.000
while a stunt person actually
steers from the driver's pod.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:55.440
This pod can be mounted
anywhere on the platform,

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:58.710
so filmmakers can basically
get any angle they want.

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:00.360
The Biscuit rig made it possible

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:02.880
to film some of Hollywood's
most famous car scenes

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:04.280
over the past two decades,

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:06.100
from this scene in "The Hangover"

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:08.700
to the many chases in "Baby Driver."

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:10.450
Take this scene in the movie "Drive,"

00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:12.920
where a guy chasing
Ryan Gosling's character

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:15.910
hits the back of his
Mustang to make it spin out.

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:18.270
After shooting the whole
thing with two stunt drivers

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:19.870
to get all the exterior shots,

00:06:19.870 --> 00:06:21.760
they filmed it again with Ryan in the car

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:23.410
and the car on the Biscuit rig

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:26.220
to get the close-up and interior angles.

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:27.811
A stunt driver drove the Biscuit

00:06:27.811 --> 00:06:29.619
while talking to Ryan through a radio

00:06:29.619 --> 00:06:31.050
to help him mimic all the movements

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:32.220
on his steering wheel.

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:33.370
In the resulting scene,

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:35.870
it looks like Ryan is in
control of the vehicle.

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:37.840
And because he is immersed in the action,

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:40.040
we as the audience are too.

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:41.530
Finding ways to make chase scenes

00:06:41.530 --> 00:06:42.910
feel more and more immersive

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:45.640
calls for increasingly
creative rig systems.

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:47.110
Like in the movie "Extraction,"

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:49.240
with a 12-minute fight and chase sequence

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:51.720
designed to look like one continuous take.

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:52.810
To pull this off,

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:56.090
director Sam Hargrave strapped
himself with his camera

00:06:56.090 --> 00:06:57.870
to the hood of a chase vehicle.

00:06:57.870 --> 00:06:59.530
From there, he could follow the action

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:01.450
from behind, unclip himself,

00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:03.630
and then move the action into the car

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:06.430
for a seamless outside-to-inside shot.

00:07:06.430 --> 00:07:09.580
This progression of bigger
and bigger chase spectacles

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:12.370
has led to the "Fast &amp; Furious" franchise,

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:15.624
where each movie tries to top
the car stunts in the last.

00:07:15.624 --> 00:07:17.660
[engines roar]

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:19.980
Today's filmmakers build on all the tools

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:21.290
we've previously seen

00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:24.140
but have to deal with a whole
other set of challenges.

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:26.670
First, cities have become more restrictive

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:29.040
about productions filming
risky chase sequences

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:30.100
in their streets,

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:32.560
so it's become commonplace to shoot chases

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:34.847
in an entirely different
city than they're set in.

00:07:34.847 --> 00:07:38.420
"Fast and Furious: Tokyo
Drift" was, despite its title,

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:39.980
mostly filmed in LA.

00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:41.780
The movie's VFX team created

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:44.540
an entirely CG Shibuya Crossing,

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:46.780
the focal point of a
major chase in the movie,

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:48.500
and used computer simulation

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:50.880
to create fake crowds and traffic.

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:53.850
Similarly, the zombie chase
in "Fate of the Furious"

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:55.400
was set in New York City

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.550
but filmed mostly in Cleveland,

00:07:57.550 --> 00:08:00.570
with digital set extensions
adding detailed architecture

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:02.480
to disguise the city streets.

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:05.080
The cars themselves are
another common hurdle.

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:07.710
Chase cars aren't built
the same way today.

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:09.260
Since the decline of the muscle car

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:10.740
beginning in the late '70s,

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:13.310
models have been getting safer and safer.

00:08:13.310 --> 00:08:15.460
Today's cars have onboard computers

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:18.010
that make them shut down
if they start to drift

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:20.140
or do a power slide or J-turn --

00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:22.070
basically, all moves
that would be dangerous

00:08:22.070 --> 00:08:23.320
for your average driver

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:25.190
but make up the basic ingredients

00:08:25.190 --> 00:08:26.567
of a Hollywood chase scene.

00:08:26.567 --> 00:08:29.060
"Black Panther"'s filmmakers
ran into this problem

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:31.110
when filming the Busan chase scene.

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:33.900
They had to have Lexus send
them a computer programmer

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:36.770
to tweak the cars'
computer systems on set,

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:39.370
overriding the many safety features.

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:41.240
This happened on "Baby Driver," too.

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:43.850
The team had to fly in a
technician from Germany

00:08:43.850 --> 00:08:45.970
to make the Mercedes-Benz slide around

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:48.730
and crash through a fence
without shutting down.

00:08:48.730 --> 00:08:52.010
This customization goes
beyond computer programs.

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:55.060
Cars have always been reinforced
and modified for stunts,

00:08:55.060 --> 00:08:58.240
but recent movies take
customization much further.

00:08:58.240 --> 00:08:59.470
For cars used in production

00:08:59.470 --> 00:09:01.160
for the "Fast &amp; Furious" movies,

00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:03.320
the crew removes the airbags from the cars

00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:05.710
and disables features
like traction control

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:07.660
and the antilock braking system.

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:10.790
They also fortify the frames
and bodies of the vehicles

00:09:10.790 --> 00:09:13.570
so they can withstand
heavy-duty stunt work.

00:09:13.570 --> 00:09:16.380
This can also mean building
new cars from scratch,

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:18.670
like the flip car that
Luke Evans' character uses

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:21.520
as a weapon in the London
chase from "Fast 6."

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:24.730
The Formula 1-style car
has a special ramp design,

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:26.770
so that when it drives into other cars

00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:28.500
it makes them go flying.

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:29.550
They also designed the car

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:32.200
with both front and
rear hydraulic steering,

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:33.160
which made it possible

00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:35.680
for the car to change
direction at high speeds

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:38.840
and look like it's gliding over the road.

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:41.470
But just as it took 100
years to get the tech

00:09:41.470 --> 00:09:42.470
to where we are now,

00:09:42.470 --> 00:09:45.970
it can still take months to
build up an epic car shot,

00:09:45.970 --> 00:09:47.410
even a short one.

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:49.080
For the upcoming "F9" movie,

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:50.220
director Justin Lin

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:53.300
said this four-second
shot with a Toyota 86

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:54.860
took eight months of prep,

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:56.190
four days of production,

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:58.470
and over 100 crew members.

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:01.290
So even if these cars are going fast,

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:03.330
it's the slow work of production

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:07.015
that's gotten chase scenes
to where they are today.

00:10:07.015 --> 00:10:12.015
[car engine sputters]
[tires squeal]

